00:00:04.309 --> 00:00:07.030 align:start position:0%
in european folklore arcs of mushrooms  known as fairy rings were considered a  portal into a different world  these mesmerizing patterns are also seen  in the grasslands of namibia  where meters wide barren circles speckle  the landscape  to ecologists these shapes are  enchanting but  not for their magical properties they  give important clues about the health of  an ecosystem  and how they might respond to change  most research on these self-organizing  patterns are in systems where fairy  circles persist long term  but in salt marshes these shapes are  part of early development  a stepping stone to establishing  widespread expanses of vegetation  now researchers have gained more insight  into these fleeting patterns  researchers used experimental testing  and mathematical modeling  to uncover how fairy circles form in  salt marshes  first they picked a young marsh to  observe they settled on the mud flats of  chongming island  and the nonhue title flats near shanghai  while marshes in many places have been  shrinking due to sea level rise  some marshes in china have expanded by  hundreds of meters a year  human development and the expansion of  farmlands pushing more sediment into the  yancey river  has expanded shorelines  during an aerial survey of the area  scientists observed rings that nestled  inside each other  and that these rings often merged in  arid environments rings repel each other  they used mathematical models to  recreate these patterns  and found two mechanisms driving them  one  the accumulation of the toxic chemical  sulfide  created when bacteria decomposes dead  plant matter  the other nutrient depletion in the  center of the rings  from heightened competition with other  plants less competition with plants  expanding outwardly  means more available nutrients to test  if these ecological processes or driving  patterns  scientists sampled from different areas  of fairy circles  the center of rings on the rings and the  bare outer edge  they found higher concentrations of  sulfide in the center samples  than on the rings and margins  although the measured amounts were not  high enough to kill plants  researchers say sampling during hotter  times of day  could yield lethal levels they did find  conclusive evidence that a lack of  nutrients played a major role in the  formation of these rings  in experiments on the salt marsh adding  nitrogen fertilizer to the center patch  increased their growth while adding it  on the rings hardly gave a boost  researchers say this study provides a  good starting point for figuring out  what could limit the expansion of  marshes in other areas  since marshes serve important functions  from buffering against storm surges  to capturing carbon understanding the  mechanisms that underlie their success  is crucial  taking a bird's-eye view of other  marshes could reveal the secrets to  their success  you
 

